        title: "Apply It: Name Your First Three Cells",
        content: `Open a worksheet you actively use at work. Find your three most-referenced "constants" — the cells everyone looks up: usually a tax rate, a markup, a target, an exchange rate, a fiscal year start, or a manager threshold.

For each one: click the cell, click the **Name Box** (top-left, left of the formula bar), type a clear name (no spaces, no leading numbers — try **TaxRate**, **AnnualTarget**, **FYStartDate**), and press Enter. Open **Name Manager** with **Ctrl + F3** to confirm all three appear.

Now open **Find & Replace** (**Ctrl + H**). Search for the cell address (like **$D$1**) and replace with the new name (**TaxRate**). Check "Match entire cell contents" off. Click **Replace All** and watch every formula update.

Save the file. Now hand the workbook to a colleague (mentally, or for real). Can they understand what **=Price*TaxRate-Discount** does without asking you? If yes, you've just upgraded your file from **a spreadsheet to a model**. That's the pro mindset. **Welcome to the next level.**`,
