      {
        type: "quiz",
        title: "Quiz: Copy Only the Filtered Rows",
        content: `You filtered a 5,000-row sales table to show only the **West region** (about 600 rows visible, the rest hidden by the filter). You select \`A2:G5000\`, press **Ctrl+C**, switch to a new sheet, and press **Ctrl+V**. To your horror, Excel pastes all **5,000 rows** including the hidden ones — your filter was useless. **Which approach gives you ONLY the 600 visible rows in the new sheet?**`,
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="55" fill="#ffd700" font-size="28" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">Quiz: Copy Only the Visible Rows</text><g transform="translate(60,100)"><rect width="980" height="260" rx="14" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="2"/><text x="490" y="40" fill="#6366f1" font-size="20" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">The Setup</text><g transform="translate(40,70)" font-family="monospace" font-size="14"><rect x="0" y="0" width="900" height="25" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#888"/><text x="20" y="17" fill="#888">Row</text><text x="100" y="17" fill="#888">Region</text><text x="300" y="17" fill="#888">Product</text><text x="500" y="17" fill="#888">Amount</text><rect x="0" y="25" width="900" height="25" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.2" stroke="#10b981"/><text x="20" y="42" fill="#ffffff">2</text><text x="100" y="42" fill="#10b981" font-weight="bold">West</text><text x="300" y="42" fill="#ffffff">Widget</text><text x="500" y="42" fill="#ffffff">$340</text><rect x="0" y="50" width="900" height="25" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#888" opacity="0.4"/><text x="20" y="67" fill="#888">3</text><text x="100" y="67" fill="#888">East (hidden by filter)</text><rect x="0" y="75" width="900" height="25" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.2" stroke="#10b981"/><text x="20" y="92" fill="#ffffff">4</text><text x="100" y="92" fill="#10b981" font-weight="bold">West</text><text x="300" y="92" fill="#ffffff">Gadget</text><text x="500" y="92" fill="#ffffff">$510</text><rect x="0" y="100" width="900" height="25" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#888" opacity="0.4"/><text x="20" y="117" fill="#888">5</text><text x="100" y="117" fill="#888">East (hidden)</text><rect x="0" y="125" width="900" height="25" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#888" opacity="0.4"/><text x="20" y="142" fill="#888">6</text><text x="100" y="142" fill="#888">South (hidden)</text><rect x="0" y="150" width="900" height="25" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.2" stroke="#10b981"/><text x="20" y="167" fill="#ffffff">7</text><text x="100" y="167" fill="#10b981" font-weight="bold">West</text><text x="300" y="167" fill="#ffffff">Doodad</text><text x="500" y="167" fill="#ffffff">$220</text></g></g><g transform="translate(60,380)" font-family="sans-serif"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><rect width="470" height="100" rx="10" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#888" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="35" fill="#ffd700" font-size="20" font-weight="bold" font-family="monospace">A</text><text x="50" y="35" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Just press Ctrl+C harder</text><text x="20" y="65" fill="#888" font-size="12">Modern Excel (2007+) actually does the</text><text x="20" y="85" fill="#888" font-size="12">right thing automatically — but only sometimes.</text></g><g transform="translate(510,0)"><rect width="470" height="100" rx="10" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.2" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="3"/><text x="20" y="35" fill="#10b981" font-size="20" font-weight="bold" font-family="monospace">B</text><text x="50" y="35" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Select range → Alt+; → Ctrl+C → paste</text><text x="20" y="65" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="bold">CORRECT — Alt+; selects visible cells only.</text><text x="20" y="85" fill="#888" font-size="12">Equivalent to F5 → Special → Visible only.</text></g><g transform="translate(0,120)"><rect width="470" height="100" rx="10" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#888" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="35" fill="#ffd700" font-size="20" font-weight="bold" font-family="monospace">C</text><text x="50" y="35" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Right-click → Paste Special → Values</text><text x="20" y="65" fill="#888" font-size="12">Strips formulas but still pastes all 5,000 rows.</text><text x="20" y="85" fill="#888" font-size="12">Solves a different problem.</text></g><g transform="translate(510,120)"><rect width="470" height="100" rx="10" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#888" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="35" fill="#ffd700" font-size="20" font-weight="bold" font-family="monospace">D</text><text x="50" y="35" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Delete hidden rows first</text><text x="20" y="65" fill="#888" font-size="12">Destructive — you'd lose the East and South data.</text><text x="20" y="85" fill="#888" font-size="12">Filter is meant to be non-destructive.</text></g></g><g transform="translate(60,640)"><rect width="980" height="440" rx="14" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2"/><text x="490" y="40" fill="#10b981" font-size="22" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">Answer: B — Alt+; then Ctrl+C</text><g transform="translate(40,70)" font-family="monospace" font-size="14"><text x="0" y="20" fill="#ffd700" font-weight="bold">The full sequence:</text><text x="0" y="55" fill="#ffffff">1. Select A2:G5000 (filtered view shows only West rows)</text><text x="0" y="80" fill="#ffffff">2. Press Alt+; (semicolon) — selects ONLY visible cells in the range</text><text x="0" y="105" fill="#ffffff">3. Press Ctrl+C — copies just the 600 visible rows</text><text x="0" y="130" fill="#ffffff">4. Switch sheets, Ctrl+V — pastes only those 600 rows</text></g><g transform="translate(40,220)" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="14"><text x="0" y="20" fill="#ffd700" font-weight="bold">Why option A is half-right but unreliable:</text><text x="0" y="50" fill="#ffffff">In modern Excel, Ctrl+C on filtered AutoFilter data often respects the filter.</text><text x="0" y="75" fill="#ffffff">But: hidden rows from manual hiding (Ctrl+9) or grouped/outlined rows still</text><text x="0" y="100" fill="#ffffff">come along. Alt+; is the bulletproof way that works in every scenario.</text></g><g transform="translate(40,360)" font-family="monospace" font-size="14"><text x="0" y="20" fill="#10b981" font-weight="bold">Alt+;  ⟺  F5 → Special → Visible cells only</text><text x="0" y="50" fill="#888">Memorize this shortcut. It pays for itself the first time you use it.</text></g></g></svg>`,
          caption: "Four candidate approaches with the correct Alt+; shortcut highlighted and the full copy-visible-only workflow explained."
        }
      },
      {
        type: "application",
        title: "The Audit Drill — Map a Stranger's Workbook in 5 Minutes",
        content: `You've just inherited a workbook from someone who left the company. Your job: understand it before changing anything. Run this **5-minute audit drill** using only selection tools. **Minute 1 — Find every formula.** Ctrl+A twice (entire sheet), F5 → Special → Formulas → OK. Excel highlights every formula. Hit Ctrl+1 → Fill tab, paint them light blue. Instant **model map**. **Minute 2 — Find every constant.** Repeat with F5 → Special → Constants → Numbers. Paint yellow. Now you can see every **hardcoded value** that *should probably* be a named input. **Minute 3 — Find blanks.** F5 → Special → Blanks. If a column has blanks where it shouldn't, you've found a data quality issue. **Minute 4 — Find errors.** F5 → Special → Formulas → Errors only. Every \`#REF!\`, \`#N/A\`, \`#DIV/0!\` lights up. Address them. **Minute 5 — Find named ranges.** Ctrl+F3 opens the Name Manager. Browse the list — it's the inherited workbook's **table of contents**. The output: a workbook you can now safely modify because you understand what each cell **is**.`,
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="55" fill="#ffd700" font-size="28" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">The 5-Minute Workbook Audit Drill</text><text x="550" y="90" fill="#888" font-size="15" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">Five passes of Go To Special turn an unknown spreadsheet into a readable model</text><g transform="translate(40,120)"><rect width="1020" height="180" rx="12" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="30" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="18" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif">MINUTE 1 — Map the Formulas</text><g transform="translate(20,45)" font-family="monospace" font-size="13"><text x="0" y="20" fill="#ffd700">Ctrl+A (twice)  →  F5  →  Special  →  Formulas  →  OK</text><text x="0" y="45" fill="#ffffff">Then: Ctrl+1 → Fill → light blue → OK</text><text x="0" y="80" fill="#0ea5e9" font-weight="bold">Outcome:</text><text x="0" y="105" fill="#ffffff">Every formula cell is now blue. You can see at a glance how the model flows —</text><text x="0" y="125" fill="#ffffff">where calculations live vs. where inputs live. This is the architecture.</text></g></g><g transform="translate(40,320)"><rect width="1020" height="180" rx="12" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="30" fill="#ffd700" font-size="18" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif">MINUTE 2 — Find the Hardcoded Constants</text><g transform="translate(20,45)" font-family="monospace" font-size="13"><text x="0" y="20" fill="#ffd700">Ctrl+A (twice)  →  F5  →  Special  →  Constants  →  Numbers only  →  OK</text><text x="0" y="45" fill="#ffffff">Then: Ctrl+1 → Fill → yellow → OK</text><text x="0" y="80" fill="#ffd700" font-weight="bold">Outcome:</text><text x="0" y="105" fill="#ffffff">Hardcoded numbers are now yellow. Any yellow cell INSIDE a formula area is a</text><text x="0" y="125" fill="#ffffff">candidate for refactoring into a named input. Magic numbers found.</text></g></g><g transform="translate(40,520)"><rect width="1020" height="180" rx="12" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="30" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="18" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif">MINUTE 3 — Find the Blanks That Shouldn't Be Blank</text><g transform="translate(20,45)" font-family="monospace" font-size="13"><text x="0" y="20" fill="#ffd700">Select the data range  →  F5  →  Special  →  Blanks  →  OK</text><text x="0" y="55" fill="#f59e0b" font-weight="bold">Outcome:</text><text x="0" y="80" fill="#ffffff">Every blank cell in your data is highlighted. Blanks in a required column =</text><text x="0" y="100" fill="#ffffff">data quality issue. Fix once: type =cell-above + Ctrl+Enter to fill them all.</text></g></g><g transform="translate(40,720)"><rect width="1020" height="180" rx="12" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="30" fill="#ef4444" font-size="18" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif">MINUTE 4 — Hunt Down the Errors</text><g transform="translate(20,45)" font-family="monospace" font-size="13"><text x="0" y="20" fill="#ffd700">Ctrl+A (twice)  →  F5  →  Special  →  Formulas  →  Errors only  →  OK</text><text x="0" y="55" fill="#ef4444" font-weight="bold">Outcome:</text><text x="0" y="80" fill="#ffffff">Every #REF!, #N/A, #DIV/0!, #VALUE!, #NAME? cell highlighted at once.</text><text x="0" y="100" fill="#ffffff">Now you know what needs IFERROR wrapping or actual debugging.</text></g></g><g transform="translate(40,920)"><rect width="1020" height="160" rx="12" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="30" fill="#10b981" font-size="18" font-weight="bold" font-family="sans-serif">MINUTE 5 — Read the Table of Contents</text><g transform="translate(20,45)" font-family="monospace" font-size="13"><text x="0" y="20" fill="#ffd700">Ctrl+F3  →  Name Manager opens</text><text x="0" y="55" fill="#10b981" font-weight="bold">Outcome:</text><text x="0" y="80" fill="#ffffff">A list of every named range and named formula. This is the inherited workbook's</text><text x="0" y="100" fill="#ffffff">vocabulary. Read it before you change ANY formula. Five minutes well spent.</text></g></g></svg>`,
          caption: "Five Go To Special passes mapped minute-by-minute, each producing a colored or listed output that explains a different aspect of the inherited workbook."
        }
      }
