        content: `A **workbook** is not one sheet — it is a *container* of sheets, each with its own grid, its own formatting, and its own role in a larger story. Pros think in terms of **sheet architecture**: an **Inputs** tab where users type, a **Calc** tab where formulas live, an **Outputs** tab that prints, and a **Lookup** tab nobody touches. This separation is the difference between a workbook a colleague can inherit and one that collapses the moment someone clicks the wrong cell. In this lesson you will learn to **add, move, copy, hide, and protect** sheets with intent — including the legendary **very hidden** trick, the **grouped-sheet** technique that edits ten tabs at once (and the disasters it causes when you forget you're grouped), and how to save your structure as a reusable **.xltx template** so version 2 starts where version 1 left off.`,
